If Jane is **not guilty**, the question is how surprising it would be to see two infant deaths in the same family with these apparent similarities.

Some similarity is **not very surprising** even under innocence:

- both babies are from the same family, so they share genetics and environment;
- both died in early infancy, so similar ages are less surprising than they would be in unrelated children;
- feeding and sleep patterns in young babies are highly repetitive, so “after being fed” and at a similar time of day may arise fairly naturally;
- the mother being alone with the baby at the critical moment may also be unsurprising if she was the primary caregiver.

So the similarities sound rhetorically powerful, but several of them are exactly the kind of common background features one would expect to repeat within the same household.

That said, **all** of these similarities occurring together does have some evidential force, so under innocence they are not maximally expected. But they are far from extraordinarily unlikely.

Overall, if Jane is not guilty, I’d judge the chance of seeing these kinds of similarities as **moderately likely**.

**Probability judgment: 60**

If Jane is **not guilty**, then it is **fairly likely** that injuries later identified on Ben were **not present, or at least not apparent/not detectable**, when he was brought to the hospital.

Why? Because under innocence, one would be more open to explanations such as:

- some findings being subtle and not readily visible to nurses on emergency admission;
- some findings being internal and only detectable at autopsy;
- some rib findings or related changes arising during resuscitation or being recognized only later on detailed examination;
- some later interpretations overstating what was actually there at presentation.

The wording matters: “not noted by nurses” does not strictly prove “not present,” but if you are asking how likely it is under innocence that they were not present at hospital arrival, I would say this is **more likely than not**, though not overwhelming.

**Probability judgment: 65**

If Jane is **guilty**, it is still **moderately possible** that the injuries later found on Ben were **not present, or not yet detectable/apparent**, when he was brought to the hospital.

That is because:

- some important findings were internal rather than obvious on routine inspection;
- nurses in an emergency setting may fail to note subtle signs;
- some rib-related findings could arise during resuscitation or only be recognized later;
- “not noted” is weaker than “definitely absent.”

However, if guilt is assumed and the injuries were inflicted before hospital arrival, then one might somewhat more often expect them to have been present already, even if unnoticed. So the proposition that they were truly not present at arrival is less natural under guilt than under innocence.

Overall, under guilt, I would judge this as **possible but not especially likely**.

**Probability judgment: 40**
